00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.050
So what I was
planning to talk about

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:23.160
is on the molecular
dynamics simulation,

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:30.450
and really how they
emphasize in the analysis

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.940
of molecular
dynamics simulation.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:40.320
So molecular dynamics simulation
conceptually is very easy.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.390
What we want is--

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:47.040
actually most of the molecular
dynamics simulation nowadays

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.300
is based on classical
molecular dynamics.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:56.440
And in fact, people
think it's done.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:00.780
So the chalk, I like
this thick chalk.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:05.510
And we'll have to
use a different one.

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:17.340
OK, so the classical molecular
dynamics is F=ma And so force

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:18.750
equals MA.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:24.210
So if I have, for
example, a simulation box,

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.040
I have a crystal.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:29.130
I want to simulate
its properties.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:32.580
And what I have,
Newton's second law

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:38.430
will tell me the mass
of the ice atom, ma,

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:45.950
so the acceleration
of the ice atom

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:50.210
equals the force
acting on the atom.

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:58.060
So we have the force of all the
other atoms j equals 1 to n.

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:03.010
The n is the number of atoms
simulating in the domain.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:09.850
And Fij, so that's the force
acting on this specific atom.

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:13.730
And, of course, j
doesn't equal to i.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.480
So that's the concept.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:17.600
It's very easy.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:24.860
And in fact, any time, say, when
new students come in and say,

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:26.320
OK, this is the easiest.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:27.400
You start from here.

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:33.640
Of course, you can think
about your task there as doing

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:40.120
integration of F=ma, and
you make sure that you have

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:44.020
the proper potential, the force.

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:51.300
Remember, the force equals
the gradient of the potential,

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:54.210
so we need a proper potential.

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:58.770
And at least
conceptually, we have

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:02.880
discussed in this course
different potentials,

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:08.800
[INAUDIBLE] potential, ionic
potentials, covalent potential.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:11.190
So all those potential
interactions.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:15.180
And the reality is, for
each different material

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:19.350
when you do classical
molecular dynamics simulation,

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:23.010
the different material--
people have spent a lot of time

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:27.000
to put the proper
potential so that they

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.290
can reproduce some of the
materials basic properties

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:32.310
like melting point.

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:36.100
When you heat it up, it melts.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.750
Or the mechanical properties.

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:42.680
And because those
are not potential

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:45.740
from first principle--
when I say first principle,

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:47.630
it's of quantum mechanics.

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:49.400
Those are the
classical potential,

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.670
and typically you
start with some concept

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:56.690
and you put in the
feeding parameters,

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:59.150
adjust the parameter
to say these

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:03.750
are the best set of parameters
for those materials.

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:06.710
But we're not getting
into discussing how

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:08.810
to construct those potentials.

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:13.760
Typically, if you are able to
construct the potential that's

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:15.590
more general, many
people used, you

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:18.800
have already achieved a
good success in this field.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:23.050
It's not easy to construct
these potentials.

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:26.830
And once you have the
potential of this second order

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:29.800
derivative, you
have the potential,

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:32.170
you do a integration.

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:36.820
And you want to
find the position

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:42.550
of the atoms, molecules,
as a function of time

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:45.190
in your simulation domain.

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:51.120
So we can do the integration.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:56.040
So, for example, the second
order derivative we know--

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.860
let's drop the vector location.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:04.350
So the second order
derivative, drop

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:11.510
the vector of i and
dt squared equals

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:15.045
ri and the t plus the other t--

00:06:15.045 --> 00:06:18.720
I use the central difference
method, for example--

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:26.640
and plus the rt it minus the
other t, match to our it.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:34.415
This is a time integration over
that 2 to the other t squared.

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:40.350
So if you go to check
the accuracy of this,

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:45.640
this is actually of the
order of the other t.

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:49.570
This is the fourth
order, so fourth power.

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:55.290
So you actually have a
relatively high accuracy

00:06:55.290 --> 00:07:00.470
when you use the central
difference method.

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:07.650
And then if I write this,
you choose a time step--

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:10.790
so this is an explicit method.

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:12.590
You choose a time step.

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:16.160
You advance it in time to
find the position of the atoms

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:17.540
at the next time.

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:19.460
So you use the--

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:21.950
so the next time
is t plus delta t.

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:25.730
You use the information previous
time step, the current time

00:07:25.730 --> 00:07:27.750
step, and previous time step.

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:28.940
So I can integrate--

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:34.130
I say I can march in
time t plus delta t.

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:39.440
And what I have here is then--

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:43.490
this one is, if you look
at the Newton second law,

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:47.010
is really just
the f divide by m.

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:58.110
So what I have here is a 2 ri
1 minus ri t minus delta t.

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:03.480
And there I have the derivative
of the acceleration, which

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:08.490
is 2 delta t squared
times the force acting

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:13.290
on this atom in that specific
direction divided by mass.

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:16.140
That's my acceleration.

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:19.680
And now, if I do this,
what I am accurate

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:22.680
is to delta t fourth power.

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:27.660
That's the terms that I dropped.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:32.490
So this is-- to
the fourth power is

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:36.350
called high numerical accuracy.

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:41.470
And what's important
is your time step.

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:45.130
You say, OK, I'm going to
accelerate my integration,

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:47.980
I choose a very large time step.

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:51.490
But remember, your
time step has to be

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:55.930
smaller than the physical
process that goes on.

00:08:55.930 --> 00:09:00.750
If I have atoms oscillate
10 to the 14th hertz,

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:05.410
that means 10 to the
minus 14 second or period.

00:09:05.410 --> 00:09:08.340
Then my delta t has
to be, if I want

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:12.150
to capture one period
of the atom oscillation,

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:13.890
you say that the
sine function, I

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:16.260
have to at least how accurate.

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:18.900
I say, OK, maybe I'll take 1/10.

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:23.520
That's not very fine
points for a sine function.

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:25.110
So you can say,
then, usually you

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:29.350
get to femtosecond,
very small time steps.

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:33.540
And because of that,
your integration time,

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:38.320
you want to simulate the
event that last a nanosecond.

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:40.480
And nanosecond, 10 to minus 9.

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:43.810
If you start from 10
to minus 15 time step,

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:47.740
you can imagine that's
a lot of marching

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:49.460
and a lot of computation.

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:55.490
And each time step, you have to
re-calculate the force because

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:58.150
of the timing position
change and that will

00:09:58.150 --> 00:10:01.160
change the force interactions.

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:03.490
So this is a time.

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:07.990
And with this, you can
also calculate velocity.

00:10:07.990 --> 00:10:17.290
Velocity for the time, t,
is just a graded derivative.

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:24.100
So we have the derivative
of for the i, the t.

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:28.090
Then I have ri t plus
the delta t which

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:31.900
I calculate in this
time And I have ri

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:34.540
t and divide it by delta t.

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:42.050
And the disadvantage
of this compared

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:46.160
to a central difference,
this accuracy is lower.

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:49.580
So only t square.

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:55.430
But it's lucky that this
doesn't quite effect--

00:10:55.430 --> 00:10:59.600
this does not, in fact, does
not affect my calculation of ri.

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:04.650
Because my ri calculation is
independent of the velocity.

00:11:04.650 --> 00:11:07.250
So my velocity is
not that accurate,

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:10.340
my position is
much more accurate.

00:11:10.340 --> 00:11:14.640
Depends on the
time step you take.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:16.830
So that's easy, right?

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:20.640
So in principle,
you can write a code

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:23.370
and do this calculation quickly.

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:27.900
And the key question is,
how do you do your analysis?

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:31.200
How you understand
your analysis?

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:34.770
Doing a numerical
simulation many times,

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:38.250
people like to say I'm doing
a numerical experiment I

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:40.620
don't like that terminology.

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:43.050
But if you are doing
computation like this,

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:46.740
say you're doing
numerical experiments,

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:51.980
the question is how you
analyze your experiment?

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:58.640
So what are we going to
discuss focus is really

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:04.700
the statistical foundation
for numerical analysis.

00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:09.470
One more comment,
before we go there,

00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:13.430
is that when you do
molecular dynamics simulation

00:12:13.430 --> 00:12:17.540
already set, your time
step has to be very short.

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:20.540
Let's say if you
do solid, you have

00:12:20.540 --> 00:12:25.050
to go to femtosecond or
even sub-femtosecond.

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:29.210
It depends on the oscillation
frequency of the atoms.

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:34.690
And the other is the number
of atoms you can simulate.

00:12:34.690 --> 00:12:39.730
And if I think I want to
simulate one micron cube.

00:12:39.730 --> 00:12:48.850
and if you say the typical,
let's say, silicon crystal.

00:12:48.850 --> 00:12:50.620
A silicon crystal
lattice constant

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:52.390
is about five Armstrong.

00:12:52.390 --> 00:12:57.360
So in a five Armstrong
cube, there are eight atoms.

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:03.000
And a one nanometer cube,
that's, say, eight times

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:04.710
eight is 64.

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:10.720
And the one micron is a
tenth to the ninth power

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:15.300
because three order of magnitude
higher in each direction.

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:18.480
And that's s 10
to nine times 64.

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:24.430
That's a number of items
you got in one micron cube.

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:27.340
And with that many
times steps, and that

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:30.340
needs very high powerful
computer to even

00:13:30.340 --> 00:13:33.190
simulate one micron.

00:13:33.190 --> 00:13:34.510
So that's very hard.

00:13:34.510 --> 00:13:40.460
And typically, people do
periodic boundary condition.

00:13:40.460 --> 00:13:42.490
So they try to similu--

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:46.870
use a much smaller
simulation box.

00:13:46.870 --> 00:13:51.460
And you have molecules
or atoms in the box.

00:13:51.460 --> 00:13:55.735
And the next moment,
this atom can move out

00:13:55.735 --> 00:13:58.510
of the simulation box.

00:13:58.510 --> 00:14:05.050
And the way that cleverly
taken into this moving out

00:14:05.050 --> 00:14:08.750
is in the opposite direction,
obviously boundaries.

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:13.340
So here it goes out of
boundary and it comes in.

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:16.760
This atom comes in
at the position,

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:19.490
at the velocity, the
corresponding position

00:14:19.490 --> 00:14:23.480
on the opposite boundary, and
the velocity that it exit.

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:27.500
So this is the trick that
people use periodic boundary

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:28.610
condition.

00:14:28.610 --> 00:14:34.310
And with that, you hope
to recapture the effects

00:14:34.310 --> 00:14:37.430
of infinite simulation.

00:14:37.430 --> 00:14:40.490
Anytime you use a purely
boundary condition,

00:14:40.490 --> 00:14:44.750
you actually say in the specific
direction where it's periodic,

00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:46.900
it's infinite.

00:14:46.900 --> 00:14:50.260
And there are also
artifacts that's

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:52.810
caused by this periodic
boundary condition

00:14:52.810 --> 00:14:57.160
as you would see much
easier once we got

00:14:57.160 --> 00:15:01.390
into the statistical aspects.

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:06.790
And let's look into the
statistical foundation.

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:29.890
And first, in chapter four,
when we do the statistics,

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:34.740
we introduce the concept
of ensemble average.

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:38.610
In the old days,
you do not actually

00:15:38.610 --> 00:15:42.510
trace the trajectory, the
time trajectory, of atoms.

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:46.290
Remember, molecular
dynamics is looking--

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:49.740
the way we describe, it is
looking to the time trajectory

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:50.860
of the atoms.

00:15:50.860 --> 00:15:52.680
And when we discuss
the statistics,

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:54.130
we said that's too complicated.

00:15:54.130 --> 00:15:56.970
That we're actually going to
do ensemble average rather than

00:15:56.970 --> 00:15:58.920
time average.

00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:03.360
And then we introduce the
concept of ergodicity,

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:06.090
our system for ergodic system.

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:09.490
The ensemble average
equals the time average.

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:14.940
Of course, the
time average, if I

00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:20.250
have a quantity, this quantity
is the quantity in phase space,

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:26.970
for example, is position and
momentum of many particles.

00:16:26.970 --> 00:16:31.530
I'm just using r
represent maybe millions

00:16:31.530 --> 00:16:36.190
or billions of atoms position
and corresponding momentum.

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:40.350
And when I do the time
average of this quantity,

00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:42.150
I say this is--

00:16:42.150 --> 00:16:46.000
I observe this quantity over a
certain period of time starting

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:47.820
any time t plus delta t.

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:49.880
So this is the origin.

00:16:49.880 --> 00:16:54.120
If I do my average correctly
and this origin doesn't matter.

00:16:54.120 --> 00:16:59.340
So here I have, this is a,
that phase space variable,

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:03.680
for example, I can write the
potential energy or kinetic

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:06.470
energy or total energy
in terms of the energy

00:17:06.470 --> 00:17:07.220
of every particle.

00:17:10.550 --> 00:17:12.569
This is the total
energy I averaged

00:17:12.569 --> 00:17:15.380
over a certain amount of time.

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:20.960
So here, this r, remember,
is a function of time.

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:23.150
Momentum is a function of time.

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:27.800
And I integrate, and
that's a definition.

00:17:27.800 --> 00:17:34.300
If I look long enough, I get
my average quantity energy

00:17:34.300 --> 00:17:37.970
or momentum or pressure.

00:17:37.970 --> 00:17:44.370
They share stress, all of
the quantities of the system.

00:17:44.370 --> 00:17:47.920
So this is the time averaging.

00:17:47.920 --> 00:17:57.680
And then I do the ensemble
average of this quantity,

00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:01.030
I use a bracket.

00:18:01.030 --> 00:18:04.300
And remember,
ensemble average is,

00:18:04.300 --> 00:18:07.780
I look at the
probability function.

00:18:07.780 --> 00:18:11.040
We see microcanonical ensemble.

00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:15.430
Each of the system in the
ensemble is equal probability.

00:18:15.430 --> 00:18:18.640
Canonical ensemble is the
Boltzmann distribution

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:24.880
or we also introduce where the
gaps in sample there where.

00:18:24.880 --> 00:18:27.490
There is not only constant
temperature, but also

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:30.640
mass flow, flux, with ambient.

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:34.630
So at the end, when I do
that the ensemble average,

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:38.440
I average over the probability.

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:45.700
So the ensemble average is
I will have this phase space

00:18:45.700 --> 00:18:49.660
variable r and p.

00:18:49.660 --> 00:18:56.470
And then I integrate over
the phase space variables.

00:18:56.470 --> 00:19:04.600
And then I have my probability
distribution four m particle

00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:12.550
in the system and r and p, and
I will-- this is my integration.

00:19:12.550 --> 00:19:16.120
When I do double
i, really mean many

00:19:16.120 --> 00:19:20.710
because the r and p is
all the particles momentum

00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:22.570
and position.

00:19:22.570 --> 00:19:26.560
So ergodic system
means my time average

00:19:26.560 --> 00:19:30.750
equals ensemble average.

00:19:30.750 --> 00:19:35.860
And in the past, we had
really follow this trajectory.

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:38.040
We were-- when I solved
Boltzmann equation,

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:43.050
I simplify this n particle
distribution function into one

00:19:43.050 --> 00:19:45.670
particle distribution function.

00:19:45.670 --> 00:19:51.220
And I do the average
velocity or heat flux

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:53.930
as we have been doing.

00:19:53.930 --> 00:19:57.290
And when you do
molecular dynamics

00:19:57.290 --> 00:20:00.080
you are simulating
time trajectory.

00:20:00.080 --> 00:20:02.210
So you're really
interested in how

00:20:02.210 --> 00:20:07.880
I'm going to do the time average
to get the quantities that I'm

00:20:07.880 --> 00:20:08.700
interested in.

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:10.220
For example, if
I want to compute

00:20:10.220 --> 00:20:15.290
the thermal conductivity,
viscosity of the system,

00:20:15.290 --> 00:20:18.800
I will look at the atomic
positions as a function of time

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:21.260
and how I can extract
this information that I'm

00:20:21.260 --> 00:20:22.500
interested in.

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:25.130
So those are the questions.

00:20:25.130 --> 00:20:28.240
And let's look at--

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:32.120
I'm interested in the property,
thermal conductivity, electric

00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:33.680
conductivity.

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:39.440
And so, in general,
those are the response

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:42.980
if I-- so let's discuss
the response function.

00:20:55.050 --> 00:20:59.070
And let me look
into a system that

00:20:59.070 --> 00:21:01.990
is initially at equilibrium.

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:03.780
So system-- equilibrium system.

00:21:08.040 --> 00:21:12.970
And a system Hamiltonian,
the Hamiltonian typically

00:21:12.970 --> 00:21:14.410
you can understand
the Hamiltonian

00:21:14.410 --> 00:21:18.500
as the kinetic energy
plus the potential energy.

00:21:18.500 --> 00:21:23.990
So the system of Hamiltonian
is H0, equilibrium.

00:21:23.990 --> 00:21:28.760
Let's say, the volume
is V. And then, I'm

00:21:28.760 --> 00:21:31.910
going to give a
disturbance to the system.

00:21:31.910 --> 00:21:33.200
I push the system.

00:21:33.200 --> 00:21:35.560
I push it every atom.

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:38.560
Or I somehow inject some heat.

00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:41.890
So right now, my disturbance
I'm going to say,

00:21:41.890 --> 00:21:45.340
I'm going to give a small
disturbance and uniform

00:21:45.340 --> 00:21:48.760
through this space.

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:50.300
And this doesn't really matter.

00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:52.480
You can extend to
a long uniform.

00:21:52.480 --> 00:22:04.440
But because I have a
perturbation due to a force.

00:22:04.440 --> 00:22:07.690
So I use a force to perturb it.

00:22:07.690 --> 00:22:11.340
And because I'm applying
force, the system Hamiltonian,

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:13.870
the energy of the
system will change,

00:22:13.870 --> 00:22:18.910
and that change is H
prime, small change.

00:22:18.910 --> 00:22:23.460
So I will say, this is the
perturbation to the Hamiltonian

00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:25.540
of the system.

00:22:25.540 --> 00:22:27.600
And I can write
this perturbation

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:29.800
because this is
the force applied.

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:38.970
So this will be related to the
position and kinetic energy

00:22:38.970 --> 00:22:41.520
of each atom in the system.

00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:45.390
So this relation,
Hamiltonian, is

00:22:45.390 --> 00:22:49.230
expressed in this perturbation.

00:22:49.230 --> 00:22:53.220
And this is the force applied.

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:58.340
And I do a normalization
to the volume.

00:22:58.340 --> 00:23:00.890
So I have my volume there.

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:02.570
And I put a negative
sign, and that

00:23:02.570 --> 00:23:06.080
doesn't matter because that
could be absorbed into any.

00:23:08.620 --> 00:23:12.940
So this is my
perturbed Hamiltonian.

00:23:12.940 --> 00:23:18.170
And now, so this
is really what is

00:23:18.170 --> 00:23:25.540
perturbing due to this force to
individual atoms or molecules.

00:23:25.540 --> 00:23:29.640
And sometime later, I want
to look at the response.

00:23:29.640 --> 00:23:33.680
I want to look at how
the heat flux change,

00:23:33.680 --> 00:23:37.390
how the shared stress change.

00:23:37.390 --> 00:23:43.000
And the shared stress, again, is
reflected by another quantity.

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:50.910
Let's say this is a B, again,
is the individual face space

00:23:50.910 --> 00:23:53.140
variable.

00:23:53.140 --> 00:23:58.220
And this one, this
is the response.

00:23:58.220 --> 00:24:01.030
This is the disturbance.

00:24:01.030 --> 00:24:07.020
So in the case where
the system is linear,

00:24:07.020 --> 00:24:10.410
my force is not very large.

00:24:10.410 --> 00:24:15.900
Then this response I know will
be proportional to the force.

00:24:15.900 --> 00:24:19.680
So this is a linear
disturbance-- linear response

00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:21.540
regime.

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:28.620
And so what I have
is, this is my force.

00:24:28.620 --> 00:24:32.430
I apply at certain time t prime.

00:24:32.430 --> 00:24:36.060
And this force could be
continuously varying,

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:37.380
time-varying.

00:24:37.380 --> 00:24:44.790
And then from the start of
time to the current time--

00:24:44.790 --> 00:24:49.953
because this r and the t
of the atoms in the system

00:24:49.953 --> 00:24:51.120
could be a function of time.

00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:53.160
It's changing, because
apply the external force.

00:24:55.900 --> 00:25:01.010
And this is just related to
a response function here.

00:25:01.010 --> 00:25:03.320
And that's a function
that I'm interested.

00:25:03.320 --> 00:25:08.090
So B, A. And this is a t
prime, this I'm looking at a t,

00:25:08.090 --> 00:25:10.530
time t, so t minus t prime.

00:25:10.530 --> 00:25:13.400
So this is at--

00:25:13.400 --> 00:25:15.560
the instant that is active.

00:25:15.560 --> 00:25:20.480
And after some time,
what's my system response?

00:25:20.480 --> 00:25:25.850
What's this response-- this
quantity is responding?

00:25:25.850 --> 00:25:29.160
It's just the only
assumption I have here

00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:31.860
is a linear small disturbance.

00:25:31.860 --> 00:25:37.050
So that this response is
proportional to the first power

00:25:37.050 --> 00:25:39.170
of my force.

00:25:39.170 --> 00:25:44.710
So here, this
would be A, say phi

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:46.600
is after effect of function.

00:25:59.100 --> 00:26:03.200
This is a general describe
what happens after apply

00:26:03.200 --> 00:26:05.040
a disturbance to the system.

00:26:05.040 --> 00:26:09.600
So it should be a real
observable-- real quantity.

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:13.383
And this is, in fact, is also
sometimes you probably some

00:26:13.383 --> 00:26:16.600
of you you heard the causality.

00:26:16.600 --> 00:26:22.420
F is the cause, and later
on it has a response.

00:26:22.420 --> 00:26:25.090
So you should not-- those
results are general.

00:26:25.090 --> 00:26:33.600
You cannot violate, and those
are the causality requirement.

00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:39.710
So what we have written
is in real time.

00:26:39.710 --> 00:26:44.050
And of course, you can always
express those properties

00:26:44.050 --> 00:26:46.220
in the frequency domain.

00:26:46.220 --> 00:26:50.390
You can do the Fourier
transform of force.

00:26:50.390 --> 00:26:57.760
And if you do your Fourier
transform, and we have here--

00:26:57.760 --> 00:27:00.830
I should do the
ensemble average.

00:27:00.830 --> 00:27:06.290
I want to look at the
Fourier transform.

00:27:06.290 --> 00:27:14.060
And then I can use the
Fourier space response.

00:27:14.060 --> 00:27:15.640
And I'm not going to derive it.

00:27:15.640 --> 00:27:17.770
But this can be proven.

00:27:17.770 --> 00:27:20.530
And at B, A--

00:27:20.530 --> 00:27:24.444
so this is the
disturbance starts with A.

00:27:24.444 --> 00:27:27.940
And this disturbed
quantity due to that force.

00:27:27.940 --> 00:27:31.400
And the response is a specific
response, for example,

00:27:31.400 --> 00:27:33.220
like I said, viscosity.

00:27:33.220 --> 00:27:36.310
So here is a frequency.

00:27:36.310 --> 00:27:42.310
And the force is also Fourier
transform and exponential i

00:27:42.310 --> 00:27:46.420
omega t and d omega.

00:27:46.420 --> 00:27:53.350
So you can say that this
susceptibility that we usually

00:27:53.350 --> 00:27:56.650
call dynamic susceptibility
of the system

00:27:56.650 --> 00:28:01.860
must be related to the
after effect function here.

00:28:01.860 --> 00:28:05.420
In fact, this susceptibility--

00:28:05.420 --> 00:28:16.740
so here we have
dynamic susceptibility

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:25.440
of the system xBA omega is
actually the Fourier transform

00:28:25.440 --> 00:28:37.360
of a-- so it's a 0 to infinite,
phi BA tau exponential minus

00:28:37.360 --> 00:28:42.900
i omega tau d tau.

00:28:42.900 --> 00:28:47.310
So if I know this, I know
the after-effect function.

00:28:47.310 --> 00:28:49.500
Or if I know
after-effect function,

00:28:49.500 --> 00:28:51.060
I know the susceptibility.

00:28:54.010 --> 00:28:59.140
And in fact, there is a
really interesting relation

00:28:59.140 --> 00:29:02.470
between this one could be
real part and imaginary part.

00:29:02.470 --> 00:29:04.880
Because I'm here I have--

00:29:04.880 --> 00:29:08.050
in the Fourier transform
I have i there.

00:29:08.050 --> 00:29:10.960
So I have a real part.

00:29:10.960 --> 00:29:13.480
I have an imaginary part.

00:29:13.480 --> 00:29:17.020
I can write, OK, I
have a real part.

00:29:17.020 --> 00:29:19.570
Plus i imaginary part.

00:29:19.570 --> 00:29:22.360
And both are
frequency dependent.

00:29:22.360 --> 00:29:24.610
You can say, because
this is really

00:29:24.610 --> 00:29:28.700
just a cosine or sine part
of the Fourier transform,

00:29:28.700 --> 00:29:29.870
if I do a cosine.

00:29:29.870 --> 00:29:34.240
So if know real part of
this frequency dependence,

00:29:34.240 --> 00:29:38.950
I can go inverse cosine
transform and get this function

00:29:38.950 --> 00:29:39.820
here.

00:29:39.820 --> 00:29:42.160
From this function, I
can do a sine transform.

00:29:42.160 --> 00:29:44.410
I can get the imaginary part.

00:29:44.410 --> 00:29:47.200
So which means the real and
imaginary part are always

00:29:47.200 --> 00:29:49.210
related.

00:29:49.210 --> 00:29:53.425
And this relation is the
Kramers-Kronig relation.

00:29:53.425 --> 00:29:54.175
It's very general.

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:07.650
If you do thermal
radiation you probably

00:30:07.650 --> 00:30:11.430
heard of, for example,
the dielectric constant

00:30:11.430 --> 00:30:14.160
or optical constant.

00:30:14.160 --> 00:30:16.360
You can calculate if
you measure real part,

00:30:16.360 --> 00:30:17.940
you can calculate
imaginary part.

00:30:17.940 --> 00:30:20.760
If you measure imaginary part,
you can calculate real part.

00:30:20.760 --> 00:30:24.550
That's example of the
Kramers-Kronig relation.

00:30:24.550 --> 00:30:30.070
And so, the real
part, for example,

00:30:30.070 --> 00:30:34.440
can be calculated
from imaginary part.

00:30:34.440 --> 00:30:40.680
And minus infinite to plus
infinite, imaginary part,

00:30:40.680 --> 00:30:43.890
omega prime.

00:30:43.890 --> 00:30:49.320
And the omega prime minus
omega d omega prime.

00:30:49.320 --> 00:30:53.100
So you can say, the
problem of this integration

00:30:53.100 --> 00:30:55.080
is when omega
prime equals omega.

00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:56.690
Because I'm
integrating from minus

00:30:56.690 --> 00:30:58.590
infinite to plus infinite.

00:30:58.590 --> 00:30:59.790
This blow up.

00:30:59.790 --> 00:31:02.160
So this has to be--

00:31:02.160 --> 00:31:03.990
this is in the front.

00:31:03.990 --> 00:31:06.730
You put a [INAUDIBLE]
principle value.

00:31:06.730 --> 00:31:11.050
So actually, there's a
negative sign for this part.

00:31:11.050 --> 00:31:14.520
And that's just
to say, when the--

00:31:14.520 --> 00:31:18.960
going from left approaching to
omega or from right approaching

00:31:18.960 --> 00:31:21.990
to omega is separated
with the limit

00:31:21.990 --> 00:31:24.720
either the singularity point.

00:31:24.720 --> 00:31:27.480
And similarly, there
is a relation--

00:31:27.480 --> 00:31:30.415
you switch-- there's
no more sign.

00:31:30.415 --> 00:31:31.290
It's a positive sign.

00:31:31.290 --> 00:31:34.350
You switch this with this,
you get another relation.

00:31:34.350 --> 00:31:38.490
So either way, if you know the
real and know imaginary part,

00:31:38.490 --> 00:31:41.370
but you need to say, if you
look at this function here,

00:31:41.370 --> 00:31:43.650
you need to know
the whole function

00:31:43.650 --> 00:31:46.170
as a function of frequency.

00:31:46.170 --> 00:31:48.720
If you just have-- if you
do an optical measurement

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:51.180
and you know only
a section of that,

00:31:51.180 --> 00:31:56.010
you may not be accurate enough
to do this inverse using

00:31:56.010 --> 00:31:59.280
the Kramers-Kronig relation.

00:31:59.280 --> 00:32:03.000
And this is a causality of what
sometimes the people say this

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:05.920
is a causality requirement.

00:32:05.920 --> 00:32:11.440
You can see that we don't
have assumptions there.

00:32:11.440 --> 00:32:16.900
And what's also
interesting, if you

00:32:16.900 --> 00:32:23.120
say this is a response on
different face-based variable.

00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:30.340
But if B is the same as A,
and the A is related to--

00:32:30.340 --> 00:32:33.100
because the A is here--

00:32:33.100 --> 00:32:37.010
is a disturbance
to the Hamiltonian,

00:32:37.010 --> 00:32:39.590
the energy of the system.

00:32:39.590 --> 00:32:43.810
So in fact, when
B equals A, there

00:32:43.810 --> 00:32:49.250
is a useful relation
that's a B equals A.

00:32:49.250 --> 00:32:54.580
And what we have is
the energy dissipated

00:32:54.580 --> 00:33:02.250
in the system due to this
external disturbance.

00:33:02.250 --> 00:33:07.110
And the E equals is related
to the imaginary part

00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:09.280
of the dynamic susceptibility.

00:33:09.280 --> 00:33:13.650
So omega over 2 V, x AA.

00:33:13.650 --> 00:33:17.460
So B equals A now,
the imaginary part.

00:33:17.460 --> 00:33:24.690
And omega F omega squared.

00:33:24.690 --> 00:33:33.290
So this is just a result out
of, again, the causality.

00:33:33.290 --> 00:33:34.685
And it's general.

00:33:37.220 --> 00:33:42.590
And here, we're discussing
the response function.

00:33:42.590 --> 00:33:44.520
And next, let's move on.

00:33:44.520 --> 00:33:51.890
So I have now the system has
a small perturbation, H prime.

00:33:51.890 --> 00:33:57.500
And initially, it was at
equilibrium situation.

00:33:57.500 --> 00:34:01.220
We have F, say the H0.

00:34:01.220 --> 00:34:03.440
And now, after
this perturbation,

00:34:03.440 --> 00:34:07.820
the distribution
function will change.

00:34:07.820 --> 00:34:12.170
Because the Hamiltonian of the
system change and distribution

00:34:12.170 --> 00:34:13.110
function will changes.

00:34:13.110 --> 00:34:18.800
So now, you see the approach I
mentioned before, one of those

00:34:18.800 --> 00:34:22.489
approaches going towards
my equation, the Fn,

00:34:22.489 --> 00:34:25.070
the n particle
distribution function

00:34:25.070 --> 00:34:28.969
is reduced to one particle
distribution function.

00:34:28.969 --> 00:34:31.909
And that's the Boltzmann
equation approach.

00:34:31.909 --> 00:34:37.250
And what we go in, in this
approach is a linearize,

00:34:37.250 --> 00:34:41.310
is actually taking the n
particle distribution function.

00:34:41.310 --> 00:34:57.990
So the linear
response theory start

00:34:57.990 --> 00:35:00.580
from the Liouville equation.

00:35:00.580 --> 00:35:03.730
And but let's say,
linearize Liouville equation

00:35:03.730 --> 00:35:06.800
but still keep the n
particle distribution.

00:35:06.800 --> 00:35:10.360
So if we go back to the
Liouville equation--

00:35:10.360 --> 00:35:12.070
and this is the
Liouville equation

00:35:12.070 --> 00:35:15.040
rated in operator form.

00:35:15.040 --> 00:35:17.410
So the Liouville
equation is an equation

00:35:17.410 --> 00:35:22.510
for the n particle distribution
function as a function of time.

00:35:22.510 --> 00:35:26.800
And we could write
this in operator.

00:35:26.800 --> 00:35:31.120
So the H, the Hamiltonian
of the system,

00:35:31.120 --> 00:35:37.540
and the Fn, this bracket is
called the Poisson bracket.

00:35:37.540 --> 00:35:41.020
I'll explain what it mean.

00:35:41.020 --> 00:35:44.300
And then say there's
also another notation.

00:35:44.300 --> 00:35:49.330
And this is using i gamma
operator and the Fn.

00:35:51.880 --> 00:35:55.480
Here, the point is I'm writing
a Liouville equation with--

00:35:55.480 --> 00:35:58.440
and this is the original
Liouville equation with the n

00:35:58.440 --> 00:36:01.470
particle distribution function.

00:36:01.470 --> 00:36:09.730
And location-wise, what we have
on the right-hand side, first

00:36:09.730 --> 00:36:21.945
so that's the curly bracket
is a Poisson bracket.

00:36:27.630 --> 00:36:31.980
And that's just a location
if you have a and b.

00:36:31.980 --> 00:36:39.360
And what it means is because
I have i particulars.

00:36:39.360 --> 00:36:42.510
So i equals 1 to--

00:36:42.510 --> 00:36:46.030
in fact, say this are the total
number of degrees of freedom.

00:36:46.030 --> 00:36:49.380
If I have capital N particle,
and each N particle,

00:36:49.380 --> 00:36:52.530
each particle has a three
degree, that's a 3N.

00:36:52.530 --> 00:36:54.510
If it's a six degree, it's a 6N.

00:36:54.510 --> 00:36:56.736
So this is a--

00:36:56.736 --> 00:37:14.070
and then I have dA and dri,
dB dpi, minus dA dpi dB dri.

00:37:17.100 --> 00:37:20.400
And with this notation,
you put it in back

00:37:20.400 --> 00:37:22.980
into the right-hand
side, that you get back

00:37:22.980 --> 00:37:25.080
to the original
Liouville equation

00:37:25.080 --> 00:37:26.670
that we have
written down before.

00:37:29.420 --> 00:37:36.320
And this one, gamma
here, is the operator.

00:37:36.320 --> 00:37:43.100
So gamma is the definition,
is the operator, is minus i,

00:37:43.100 --> 00:37:48.775
and the H, and whatever behind
this function is put in here.

00:37:48.775 --> 00:37:50.025
This is the operator notation.

00:37:53.260 --> 00:37:57.690
And the use of-- this is
the Liouville operator.

00:37:57.690 --> 00:37:59.850
The use of this
Liouville operator

00:37:59.850 --> 00:38:03.330
is now with this
written as this form,

00:38:03.330 --> 00:38:08.590
you can just think of this as an
ordinary differential equation.

00:38:08.590 --> 00:38:11.620
And you have to go in back
into all this detailed math.

00:38:11.620 --> 00:38:13.990
But think this is the ordinary
differential equation.

00:38:13.990 --> 00:38:17.060
You solve it as the
